 of vertical lines needed, maybe use some horizontal or diagonal lines to cover multiple points, allowing more sunny lines. But we need exactly 2 sunny lines.\n\nWe need to see if any configuration with exactly 2 sunny lines exists. The solution's argument: For k ≤ 2, they argue that column x=n-2 (size 3) cannot be covered solely by sunny lines, so need a vertical line at x=n-2. Then they argue about column x=n-1 (size 2) and column x=n (size 1). They claim that if we don't use vertical line at x=n-1, then both points must be covered by sunny lines, each sunny line must contain exactly one of them. Then they argue that (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with a point of column n-1 because any line joining (n,1) with (n-1,b) is either horizontal (if b=1) or slope -1 (if b=2?). Let's check: Points in column n-1 are (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). The line through (n,1) and (n-1,1) is horizontal (y=1). That's non-sunny. The line through (n,1) and (n-1,2) has slope (2-1)/(n-1-n) = 1/(-1) = -1. That's slope -1, also non-sunny. So indeed (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with any point from column n-1. So (n,1) must be covered by a sunny line that does not contain any point from column n-1. But we have only 2 sunny lines total. One of them might be used to cover a point from column n-2? Let's see.\n\nWe have column n-2 with points (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3). Since we need a vertical line at x=n-2 (non-sunny) to cover them because k ≤ 2, we have that vertical line. So we have one vertical line at x=n-2. Then we have n-3 other non-sunny lines to allocate. We need to cover column n-1 (2 points) and column n (1 point). We have 2 sunny lines to use. The argument says we cannot cover column n-1 with a vertical line (since we already used vertical line at x=n-2, we could also use vertical line at x=n-1, but that would be a non-sunny line, which would increase non-sunny count but we have n-2 non-sunny lines total, so we could use vertical line at x=n-1 as well. But the argument says we cannot because we need exactly k=2 sunny lines, but we can have any number of non-sunny lines up to n-2. So we could use vertical line at x=n-1 as a non-sunny line. That would cover both points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). Then we would need to cover (n,1) with a sunny line. That would be okay: we have 2 sunny lines, one could be used for (n,1) and maybe also cover some other points. So k=2 might be possible: Use vertical lines at x=1,...,x=n-2 (that's n-2 vertical lines). Then we have used n-2 lines already, all non-sunny. We need total n lines, so we have 2 more lines, which must be sunny. We need to cover points (n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1). But we have no non-sunny lines left (since we used n-2 non-sunny lines). Actually we used n-2 vertical lines, which is exactly the allowed number of non-sunny lines (n-k = n-2). So we cannot add any more non-sunny lines. So we must cover the remaining points with the 2 sunny lines. So we need to cover (n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1) with 2 sunny lines. Is that possible? Let's see.\n\nWe have 3 points to cover with 2 sunny lines. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column (since they are not vertical). So a sunny line can cover at most one point from column n-1 and at most one from column n. So we need at least 2 sunny lines to cover the 2 points in column n-1 (since each sunny line can cover at most one point from that column). So we need exactly 2 sunny lines to cover both points in column n-1. Then (n,1) must be covered by one of those sunny lines as well, but a sunny line can cover at most one point from column n (since column n has only one point). So one of the sunny lines could cover (n,1) and also one of the points from column n-1? But we just argued that any line joining (n,1) with a point from column n-1 is either horizontal or slope -1, which are non-sunny. So a sunny line cannot contain both (n,1) and any point from column n-1. So (n,1) must be covered by a sunny line that does not contain any point from column n-1. But we have only 2 sunny lines, and both must be used to cover the two points in column n-1 (each covering one). So there is no sunny line left to cover (n,1). Therefore impossible. So k=2 is impossible.\n\nThus the argument seems valid.\n\nNow, what about k≥4? The solution says that having k≥4 would force at least four columns to rely on sunny lines for at least one of their points, leaving at most n-4 vertical lines to cover the remaining columns. Because the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column n-2, we would run out of vertical lines before all columns are covered. Let's examine this more carefully.\n\nWe have n lines total, k sunny, n-k non-sunny. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. Horizontal and slope -1 lines also meet each column at most one point (since they are not vertical). So only vertical lines can cover multiple points in a column. So to cover a column with more points than the number of sunny lines that can be assigned to that column, we need vertical lines.\n\nSpecifically, each column c has h_c = n+1-c points. Sunny lines can cover at most one point per column each. So the total number of points in column c that can be covered by sunny lines is at most k (if each sunny line picks a point in that column). But we have only k sunny lines total, and they can be assigned across columns. So for each column, the number of points covered by sunny lines is at most min(k, h_c). But we need to cover all points. The remaining points must be covered by non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical lines covering entire columns, or horizontal/diagonal lines covering at most one point per column.\n\nThus, to cover a column with h_c points, we need at least h_c - (max number of sunny lines assigned to that column) vertical lines? Actually, we can also use horizontal lines that cover points across columns, but each horizontal line covers at most one point per column (since each horizontal line is y = constant, and each column has at most one point at that y). So horizontal lines also cannot cover multiple points in the same column. Similarly for slope -1 lines. So only vertical lines can cover multiple points in a column. So to cover column c, we need at least h_c - s_c vertical lines, where s_c is the number of sunny lines that cover points in column c. Since each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, s_c ≤ k, but also s_c ≤ h_c. So we need at least h_c - min(k, h_c) vertical lines for column c. But vertical lines are whole columns, so each vertical line covers an entire column. So we need to allocate vertical lines to columns. If we allocate a vertical line to column c, it covers all points in that column, so we don't need any sunny lines for that column. So we can think of covering columns either by a vertical line (non-sunny) or by using at most one point per column covered by sunny lines (or horizontal/diagonal lines). But we have only n-k non-sunny lines, which can be vertical, horizontal, or diagonal. However, horizontal and diagonal lines cannot cover multiple points in a column, so they are not efficient for covering columns with many points. So to cover columns with many points, we need vertical lines.\n\nThus, the number of vertical lines needed is at least the number of columns c such that h_c > k (since if a column has more points than the number of sunny lines, we cannot cover all points in that column using sunny lines only; we need at least one vertical line for that column). Actually, we need at least one vertical line for each column where h_c > k, because we cannot assign more than k sunny lines to that column (since each sunny line can cover at most one point per column). So if h_c > k, we need a vertical line for that column. So the number of required vertical lines is at least the number of columns with h_c > k.\n\nNow, h_c = n+1-c. So h_c > k means n+1-c > k => c n+1 - k. So columns with index c ≤ n - k (since c